{"id": "mathvision-10", "source": "mathvision", "lang": "en", "band": "nivel2", "family": null, "answer": "10", "pred": "16", "correct": false, "boxed": true, "lang_ok": true, "reply": "To solve this, let's first understand the seating arrangement.\n\n- The original table is square and can seat 4 people.\n- When we push four such tables together in a row, we are essentially combining four tables into one long row.\n- The key is to determine how many people can be seated in a row of four tables.\n\nEach table can seat 4 people. So, if we have 4 tables, the total number of people that can be seated is:\n\n$$ 4 \\text{ tables} \\times 4 \\text{ people per table} = 16 \\text{ people} $$\n\nSo, the maximum number of people that can be seated is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{16} $$"}
